// Source: Genie Vina.pdf, Step 1 ERD Design, table 3 (classification_rules)
// Spec text (verbatim): "id, classificationName, description,
// judgmentCriteria, createdAt"

// [AI] RESTRUCTURED after reading Genie Vina.pdf. The previous version of this
// file was a keyword-matching table (@@unique([keyword, targetClassification]),
// i.e. MANY rows per classification). The PDF settles that this is wrong:
//   - Step 2 lists exactly one endpoint, "GET /api/classification-rules —
//     Get classification criteria".
//   - "[ Standard Classification Criteria ]" then lists exactly FIVE entries,
//     each a name + a one-line description:
//       Outsourcing Vendor  : software outsourcing or project-based development
//       SI Company          : enterprise system implementation, integration...
//       Product Company     : owns SaaS, platform, solution, or software product
//       Consulting/IT Service : IT consulting, operation, project management...
//       Specialized Tech Vendor : AI, Cloud, Data, Cybersecurity, Blockchain
// That maps 1:1 onto (classificationName, description) — so this table is a
// CATALOG with one row per classification, and classificationName is @unique.
// A keyword-per-row design would have made GET /api/classification-rules
// return dozens of keyword rows instead of the five documented criteria.
model ClassificationRule {
  // [AI] BigInt autoincrement per the ERD ("id bigint [pk, increment]"),
  // consistent with vendor_sources / vendor_summaries.
  id BigInt @id @default(autoincrement())

  // [AI] The ERD's "classificationName", kept under the spec's own name this
  // time. Typed as the VendorClassification enum rather than the ERD's literal
  // `varchar` — the five enum values in enums.prisma already match the five
  // criteria in the PDF exactly, and an enum means a rule can't name a
  // classification that vendors.classification could never hold.
  // @unique enforces the "one row per criterion" reading above.
  classificationName VendorClassification @unique

  // [AI] ERD "description" — the PDF's one-line summary of the criterion.
  description String? @db.VarChar(500)

  // [AI] ERD "judgmentCriteria" — prose stating what evidence justifies this
  // classification. Backs Step 3.4: "Classification must be based on visible
  // evidence such as service description, technology stack, industry
  // experience, and company profile."
  judgmentCriteria String? @db.VarChar(1000)

  // [AI] NOT in the ERD. The machine-readable form of judgmentCriteria: the
  // tokens rule-matcher.service.ts actually searches for. Kept as a scalar
  // list on the criterion row (rather than the previous one-row-per-keyword
  // design) so the table still reads as the five-row catalog the PDF
  // describes. ClassificationRulesService.match() expands each row into one
  // MatchableRule per keyword before calling the matcher.
  // -> MENTION TO TEAM: this is the one field here with no basis in the PDF or
  //    the ERD. It exists to keep rule-matcher.service.ts (already written and
  //    unit-tested by My) usable. Drop it and the auto-match endpoint goes too.
  keywords String[]

  // [AI] NOT in the ERD either. Tie-break inputs for
  // RuleMatcherService.compareRules(), which sorts priority ASC, then weight
  // DESC, then createdAt, then id. Without them a vendor matching two criteria
  // resolves non-deterministically, which Step 3.4's "must be based on visible
  // evidence" cannot tolerate.
  priority Int @default(100)
  weight   Int @default(1)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  // [AI] NOT in the ERD (which gives this table createdAt only). Kept because
  // a criterion genuinely is edited in place — unlike vendor_summaries, which
  // is append-only for exactly that reason.
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  // [AI] isActive / deletedAt from the earlier version were DROPPED. They only
  // existed to soft-delete keyword rows; against a fixed five-row catalog
  // keyed uniquely by classificationName, a deleted criterion is simply
  // re-created with the same name, and the soft-delete-vs-unique-index
  // conflict that needed a "revive" branch disappears entirely.
  // -> MENTION TO TEAM: this means DELETE is a hard delete. It is ADMIN-only.

  @@index([priority])
  @@map("classification_rules")
}
